workedExamples.question_text:
Use the area model to calculate $267\times4$. Then check the product by partitioning $267$ as $260+7$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont},draw=black]
\draw (0,0) rectangle (5.4,1.5);
\draw (2.2,0)--(2.2,1.5);
\draw (3.8,0)--(3.8,1.5);
\node[above] at (1.1,1.5) {$200$};
\node[above] at (3,1.5) {$60$};
\node[above] at (4.6,1.5) {$7$};
\node[left] at (0,0.75) {$4$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

workedExamples.solution_text:
The place-value parts are $200$, $60$ and $7$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},draw=black]
\draw (0,0) rectangle (5.4,1.5);
\draw (2.2,0)--(2.2,1.5);
\draw (3.8,0)--(3.8,1.5);
\node[above] at (1.1,1.5) {$200$};
\node[above] at (3,1.5) {$60$};
\node[above] at (4.6,1.5) {$7$};
\node[left] at (0,0.75) {$4$};
\node at (1.1,0.75) {$800$};
\node at (3,0.75) {$240$};
\node at (4.6,0.75) {$28$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
$\begin{align*}200\times4&=800\\60\times4&=240\\7\times4&=28\end{align*}$
$\begin{aligned}&\mathrel{\phantom{=}}267\times4\\&=800+240+28\\&=1068\end{aligned}$
For the different partition, $267=260+7$.
$260\times4=1040$ and $7\times4=28$.
$1040+28=1068$. Both partitions give the same product.

workedExamples.question_text:
Use the area model to calculate $1406\times7$. Explain why there is no rectangle for the tens part.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont},draw=black]
\draw (0,0) rectangle (5.4,1.5);
\draw (2.2,0)--(2.2,1.5);
\draw (3.8,0)--(3.8,1.5);
\node[above] at (1.1,1.5) {$1000$};
\node[above] at (3,1.5) {$400$};
\node[above] at (4.6,1.5) {$6$};
\node[left] at (0,0.75) {$7$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

workedExamples.solution_text:
$1406=1000+400+0+6$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},draw=black]
\draw (0,0) rectangle (5.4,1.5);
\draw (2.2,0)--(2.2,1.5);
\draw (3.8,0)--(3.8,1.5);
\node[above] at (1.1,1.5) {$1000$};
\node[above] at (3,1.5) {$400$};
\node[above] at (4.6,1.5) {$6$};
\node[left] at (0,0.75) {$7$};
\node at (1.1,0.75) {$7000$};
\node at (3,0.75) {$2800$};
\node at (4.6,0.75) {$42$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
$\begin{align*}1000\times7&=7000\\400\times7&=2800\\6\times7&=42\end{align*}$
$\begin{aligned}&\mathrel{\phantom{=}}1406\times7\\&=7000+2800+42\\&=9842\end{aligned}$
The tens part is $0$, and $0\times7=0$. It adds nothing to the product, so the model has only the three nonzero parts.

